  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Talc processing plant Nangarhar province</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Note: This project has been cancelled, because the local security situation prevented the implementation of the project.The local partner presently mixes paints for the exterior of buildings using ingredients imported from Pakistan. Their brand successfully competes with other paints on quality. They produce 4,200 Mtons per year. Their margin is very slim and they have looked for possibilities to reduce production costs. In their kind of paint 50% of the price is constituted by titanium oxide. This component can be substituted partly by using talc. In a neighbouring district very high quality unprocessed talc (98% purity) is excavated and sold to China and Pakistan. The processed talc imported from Pakistan is of very poor quality and expensive.The local partner decided to use the Afghan talc and process it themselves. In Pakistan they met with a manager of a Swiss company specialised in minerals. He introduced the local partner to the applicant, an Austrian company that processes talc. This company is desperate to find a new supply of talc as the major exporters, China and Pakistan, have reduced their exports with 25% and process it themselves.The partners decided to start a joint venture buying lumps of talc from the miners in Afghanistan and process it in Afghanistan. 60% will be transported to the applicant, 40% will be used for paint production by the local partner. The applicant will process it further for all kind off purposes like ingredients for food and pharmaceuticals. If successful, the joint venture will excavate the talc themselves and increase the production. They have already agreed this with the Afghan company supplying the talc.</narrative>
  </description>
